WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.960
One of the most controversial and often maligned&nbsp;
mechanics anywhere in the commander format is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.200
Eminence. Eminence is a keyword officially on five&nbsp;
legendary creatures and unofficially on a sixth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.760
and its effect is simple—it lets one line of&nbsp;
text on a commander also work while the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.480
is in the command zone. So, Sidar Jabari of&nbsp;
Zhalfir’s first line of text allows you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.400
loot—to draw and then discard—whenever you&nbsp;
attack with one or more knights, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.040
happens regardless of whether he himself is on&nbsp;
the battlefield or in the command zone. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.520
he has another line of text that only applies&nbsp;
while he’s on the field and plays off the eminence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.560
line—reanimating a Knight creature whenever he&nbsp;
deals combat damage to an opponent. This is how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.120
all 6 of the Eminence creatures work—there will&nbsp;
be the eminence ability that helps the deck a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.360
bit earlier in the game, and then the commander&nbsp;
will be a sort of followup to that once played,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.120
amplifying the advantage gained by the&nbsp;
eminence ability. If you’re somebody who’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.600
totally new to the keyword, this could seem like&nbsp;
a pretty reasonable sort of ability structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.880
a relatively unobjectionable way for a&nbsp;
commander to be designed. And, in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.720
I’m going to argue just that—mostly. Eminence&nbsp;
is a largely fine keyword that I would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.840
see again in some form, and its bad reputation&nbsp;
is primarily due to some questionable design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.560
decisions on Wizards of the Coast’s part when&nbsp;
they designed some of the original eminence cards.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.080
There are a lot of places I could start my&nbsp;
daring defense, but I’m going to start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.440
two interesting things that happened this year.&nbsp;
In May, Atraxa Praetors’ Voice lost her position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.720
as the most popular commander. Then, last&nbsp;
month, she lost her position as the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:37.760
most popular commander as well. What replaced her&nbsp;
was The Ur Dragon and Edgar Markov, both with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.560
keyword “eminence”. If you’re unfamiliar with&nbsp;
these, you’re probably asking, what could these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.560
commanders do to make them this popular? And,&nbsp;
let’s start there. Edgar Markov’s eminence ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.000
reads “Whenever you cast another vampire spell,&nbsp;
create a 1/1 black vampire creature token.” More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.240
mid-powered Edgar decks will typically run a wide&nbsp;
range of different mana values and enjoy the extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.480
vampire every turn or two, whereas higher-powered&nbsp;
Edgar decks will blast the table with an endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.560
wave of low-cost vampires to take advantage of&nbsp;
the complete lack of any restrictions on Eddie’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.760
eminence ability, as well as the plentiful&nbsp;
go-wide payoffs Mardu vampires has access to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.040
Edgar the playable creature is a 6 mana 4/4&nbsp;
with first strike and haste that gives all your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:23.600
vampires a +1/+1 counter when he attacks. Not a&nbsp;
bad effect, but certainly not something most Edgar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.040
players will be rushing to spend 6 mana to get.&nbsp;
What about the Ur Dragon, the current most popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.800
commander? His eminence ability simply reads&nbsp;
“your other dragon spells cost 1 less to cast.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.640
Unlike Edgar Markov, The Ur-Dragon’s creature&nbsp;
form is quite good if you have the mana for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.520
with him being a 9 mana 10/10 flier with the&nbsp;
ability “Whenever one or more dragons you control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.800
attack, draw that many cards, then you may put&nbsp;
a permanent card from your hand onto the field.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.440
This collective package doesn’t require much&nbsp;
explanation—the Eminence ability is a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.720
mana discount for dragons and the field ability is&nbsp;
a powerful value engine with dragons, collectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.840
producing the two primordial resources for&nbsp;
generically-playing-the-game as a dragon deck.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.880
So these are our two most popular commanders&nbsp;
at present day. They’re also controversial and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.440
disliked by a lot of players, particularly&nbsp;
Edgar Markov. I am among those players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.680
and I don’t think either of these cards should&nbsp;
have been printed in the form they were. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.440
the reasons why these cards are seen as&nbsp;
problematic by me and some other players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.000
are complicated and have layers to them. The&nbsp;
simplest answer is because they’re powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.120
and I actually would consider this a major&nbsp;
feature in what makes these cards problematic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.240
However, power alone doesn’t make a commander&nbsp;
an issue. Sisay, Weatherlight Captain is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.080
of the most powerful commanders in the game,&nbsp;
but I don’t think most people would consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.720
her any sort of problem. She operates in a&nbsp;
unique way and within a niche that has other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.360
good options for players who want a different&nbsp;
playstyle, and her cEDH variants don’t tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.480
bleed play patterns into casual in the same way&nbsp;
some other commanders do. So what makes these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.240
weaker commanders more of an issue by comparison?&nbsp;
One of these is simple—being associated with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.880
iconic creature type imbues them with a natural&nbsp;
casual commander aura—a tutor engine like Sisay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.840
obviously feels weird at a table of precons,&nbsp;
but that big ol’ dragon deck might pass the vibe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.320
check just fine. This is the classic feature of a&nbsp;
pubstomper commander—not strictly being powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.080
but being out of place, easily wriggling its&nbsp;
way into games it might not really belong in.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.320
Another power-associated issue I’d identify&nbsp;
with these is something I alluded to when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.840
talking about Sisay earlier—these two crowd&nbsp;
other commanders out of lanes. Dragons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.160
vampires are two of the most iconic Magic: the&nbsp;
Gathering creature types, and they’re naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.560
going to be popular choices for players new&nbsp;
to the commander format. The issue, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.440
is that both of these commanders ended up being by&nbsp;
far the strongest option for their creature type,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.760
and that fact then pushed any new Dragon&nbsp;
or Vampire player looking for a shortcut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.160
to a strong deck to get there by paying&nbsp;
a lot of money for the singular strongest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.200
commander. Edgar Markov spent over four&nbsp;
years above the $50 mark and the Ur Dragon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.720
spent two of those years at a similar price,&nbsp;
with both of their peak periods corresponding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.880
with the surge in the popularity of the&nbsp;
commander format that started in late 2020.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.280
Initially, the connection with eminence might&nbsp;
seem pretty surface level—a powerful eminence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.680
card takes over a creature type, and a weaker&nbsp;
eminence card might not do this. But this misses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.040
a key feature, which is how eminence interacts&nbsp;
with colors. See, the Ur-Dragon’s color identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:29.120
is part of its issue—if Ur Dragon had the same&nbsp;
eminence text but was mono red or even red blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.440
suddenly that’s an actual niche. Bladewing&nbsp;
could have stayed the red black choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.680
Atarka could have stayed the red green choice,&nbsp;
and the ever-funky Scion of the Ur Dragon could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.480
have stayed the choice for players&nbsp;
who really wanted all five colors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.960
But, if you instead make a 5 color option&nbsp;
that’s dramatically better than the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.720
and if that power doesn’t even require casting&nbsp;
the card, you destroy a lot of the reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.040
consider other options. Even if you don’t&nbsp;
want to play with a five color manabase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.760
the discount alone makes Ur-Dragon already better&nbsp;
than most of the other options even if it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:05.920
never cast. The same goes for Edgar—vampires are&nbsp;
traditionally mono black, followed by black red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.360
followed by black white. If Edgar had been&nbsp;
put in the relatively-unexplored-in-2017 color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.760
identity of black white, suddenly vampire&nbsp;
players would have a choice between the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:21.040
established vampire identity of black red and&nbsp;
the newer, Ixalan-centered identity of black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.720
white with this spicy new commander. Instead,&nbsp;
both of these are slurped up by Edgar, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.560
fact that most of his power is in his eminence&nbsp;
ability means that players don’t even need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.280
try to be in three colors—just stick him at the&nbsp;
head of your red black vampire deck and cast him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.400
when you draw your command tower, and bing bang&nbsp;
boom, your deck just received a strict upgrade.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.000
To be clear, I don’t think it’s wrong for&nbsp;
Wizards of the Coast to have wanted big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.960
powerful uniting options with lots of colors&nbsp;
to hand to some iconic creature types. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:53.840
Eminence has some unique problems in fitting this&nbsp;
role, and for this reason that I would lean toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.840
two colors or less on eminence commanders as&nbsp;
a rule, especially if they’re in a niche where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.080
they risk overshadowing other options. I also&nbsp;
might have done this with the more recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.640
printed Sidar Jabari of Zhalfir which works with&nbsp;
Knights, though at least that one has a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.360
balance between the power of the eminence ability&nbsp;
and the power of the castable creature. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:17.120
it might sound like this “niche” element—being&nbsp;
tied to iconic creature types—is a big part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.720
my problem with Edgar and the Ur Dragon. And,&nbsp;
in a sense it is, but tying eminence cards to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.760
a creature type actually accomplishes an&nbsp;
important goal—increasing the specificity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.560
of eminence abilities. See, having an effect&nbsp;
baked into your gameplay for free is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.040
potent. Some players might find it inherently&nbsp;
problematic, and my personal opinion is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.960
those players are underestimating the sheer power&nbsp;
that a traditional command zone can offer a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.760
but still. Getting an effect without paying for&nbsp;
it is certainly notable, and it’s important that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.720
this effect is specific so as to contain how&nbsp;
out of hand it can get. This is true for both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.440
the trigger of the eminence ability and the&nbsp;
payoff for it—ideally it should be a specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.680
trigger that provides a specific output, but&nbsp;
if you’re going to make one a bit more general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.360
you really need to be careful to crank up the&nbsp;
specificity of the other. Let’s take the example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.640
of an eminence ability that gets this right&nbsp;
in my book: that of Arahbo, Roar of the World.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.800
On paper this ability is by no means weak—giving&nbsp;
+3/+3 to a creature every combat step from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.440
very first turn of the game is quite powerful,&nbsp;
allowing for some hefty beatdown early and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.320
amplifying later damage done with various effects&nbsp;
that play off of damage dealt. However, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.720
effect, in addition to typically falling under&nbsp;
the generally less problematic aggro archetype,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.640
is specific. It’s an effect that makes a cat&nbsp;
more powerful at attacking. There’s a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.320
double strike cats and a singular infect&nbsp;
cat that will offer the occasional early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.960
kill on a blockerless opponent, but with the cat&nbsp;
limitation you don’t have access to a whole lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.160
of cards that would be powerful if they were a&nbsp;
lot bigger on an early turn. This specificity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.720
counterintuitively has a diversifying effect&nbsp;
on the play patterns of Arahbo decks—rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:58.000
than being all-in on taking advantage of the +3/+3&nbsp;
buff on turns 2-4 when it’s the most powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.560
Arahbo players need to devise ways to make use of&nbsp;
the effect more holistically throughout the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.880
and this can take a variety of different forms.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:10.400
Arahbo’s eminence is also uniquely interactable&nbsp;
among eminence commanders—a sorcery-speed +3/+3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.840
pump doesn’t typically do anything until creatures&nbsp;
are making contact during combat, which leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.040
ample opportunity to interact with it. I like&nbsp;
this design for Arahbo, but you may have noticed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:27.200
that I did say “uniquely interactable”. All other&nbsp;
Eminence abilities are easier to get value with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:32.800
with Sidar Jabari only requiring attacking with&nbsp;
knights, Inalla only requiring Wizards to enter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:36.960
and the other three being almost entirely&nbsp;
impossible to stop the effects of. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.800
un-interactibility is another point of critique&nbsp;
that you’ll most commonly find from the removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.480
lovers of the world—these commanders all&nbsp;
have effects that can’t even be gotten rid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:49.120
of by dusting them with Swords to Plowshares.&nbsp;
Now, I’ll lay my cards on the table here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.840
say that in principle this feature of Eminence&nbsp;
doesn’t strictly bother me. There are lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.920
things that can’t be interacted with barring hard&nbsp;
stax pieces—drawing a card at the start of turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.320
playing lands, untapping permanents, and so forth.&nbsp;
This game is also full of hexproof creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:07.360
uncounterable spells, and abilities that can’t&nbsp;
even be responded to. Setting these more specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.520
examples aside, it’s also important to remember&nbsp;
that deleting opposing stuff doesn’t need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.480
the end-all-be-all of EDH, and I find that this&nbsp;
intense focus on spot removal tends to indicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.960
a lack of creativity from players. A couple&nbsp;
months ago I discussed on a 2nd channel video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.600
that I actually enjoy not having many tools to&nbsp;
deal with very large creatures in one of my decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.160
as it forces me to think harder with my attacks&nbsp;
and utilize the deck’s more aggressive tools in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.800
some situations. This is a game with many&nbsp;
axes on which to attack opposing resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.560
whether that be board, hand, library, life&nbsp;
total, or the stack, and it’s good to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.480
willing to move outside your comfort zone&nbsp;
sometimes among those different options.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.080
Now, does all of this mean that the&nbsp;
un-interactability of Eminence creatures is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.760
never a problem? No, and in practice this feature&nbsp;
of eminence can be a real pain point for players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.400
with Edgar Markov being one of the more egregious&nbsp;
examples of an eminence ability feeling bad due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.520
to its un-interactibility. His eminence ability&nbsp;
triggers on-cast for a card type with a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.400
great low-cost options, any number of times per&nbsp;
turn. If I’m at a low life total and I counter two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:10.480
vampire spells that the Edgar player plays, they&nbsp;
still get the two tokens. If they have a source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:15.920
of haste—Edgar is in red, remember—these vampires&nbsp;
can attack immediately. And these easy-to-obtain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.720
and hard-to-prevent 1/1s aren’t just creatures&nbsp;
to attack and block with. Vampires as a type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.960
have a bunch of really powerful cards that take&nbsp;
advantage of the raw number of vampires in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.040
most of which can only be prevented with a&nbsp;
counterspell, and that’s to say nothing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.040
the Blood Artists and other aristocrat tools&nbsp;
that powerful Eddie decks tend to be crammed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.520
with. An eminence ability that grants creature&nbsp;
tokens for casting spells was always going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:41.440
be a little tricky to pull off regardless&nbsp;
of creature type, and that ability being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:46.320
for vampires in a deck with black puts fully&nbsp;
over the line into dangerous territory—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:49.440
tokens are too easy to get, and there’s&nbsp;
too many things that can be done with them.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:53.200
In this extended discussion of how Edgar&nbsp;
plays, the point I’m trying to make is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.200
that the frustrating play patterns of him and&nbsp;
some other Eminence commanders are the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.560
of specific design choices, not a problem with&nbsp;
the eminence mechanic as a whole. Gavin Verhey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.480
who worked on the design for Edgar Markov and&nbsp;
other eminence cards, has said that future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:11.920
Eminence designs should avoid generating permanent&nbsp;
on-board material for players. I personally would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.640
set a similar but somewhat broader rule—the&nbsp;
difficulty and specificity of the setup needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.520
to match the power of the payoff. If triggering&nbsp;
an eminence ability is easy, the payoff should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.840
be less generic and require more ingenuity to&nbsp;
take advantage of, as is the case with Oloro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:30.800
and to a lesser extent Inalla. If an eminence&nbsp;
ability has a stronger and more generic payoff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:34.000
it should have a triggering condition&nbsp;
that is more specific or more preventable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:40.240
as is true of Arahbo. When in doubt, designers&nbsp;
should go more specific with both of these things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.400
Relating back to Gavin’s view, giving players&nbsp;
something like a creature token or a treasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.760
token is an exceptionally generic payoff,&nbsp;
and engineering a condition that’s adequately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:55.440
specific to avoid the card feeling frustrating&nbsp;
or uninteractible is going to be a big challenge.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.680
I also choose a broader rule here because&nbsp;
I think The Ur Dragon is an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:05.040
frustratingly uninteractible design as well,&nbsp;
though to a lesser extent than Edgar. Mana is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:09.920
one of the two primordial resources of Magic: the&nbsp;
Gathering, and discounting creatures is equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:14.240
to giving players an Eldrazi Scion worth of&nbsp;
mana to spend every time they’d want to cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.560
one of those creatures. The Medallion cycle,&nbsp;
which each discount spells of a given color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:23.360
are some of the most powerful 2 mana artifacts&nbsp;
in the game, and The Ur-Dragon puts a roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:28.640
equivalent discount in the command zone. So, a&nbsp;
Dragon deck can get an entirely impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:32.640
stop accelerant to their gameplan, and the rest&nbsp;
of the table can only do their best to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.600
the results of that acceleration. This can pose&nbsp;
a problem for players having fun in the here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:42.080
now, but the much larger problem is with what it&nbsp;
does to the design space of the game, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:47.360
is another thing that comes up in the Commander&nbsp;
Chronicles episode. Dragons might cost a lot now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:52.480
but making a card type all cost 1 less straight&nbsp;
from the command zone with no restrictions means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:57.680
that any low-cost cards of that type in the&nbsp;
future that include any colorless are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:03.040
have an outsized impact on Ur-Dragon decks. WotC&nbsp;
has kind of fully committed to being careful with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:08.080
this specifically with dragons since they also&nbsp;
recently printed Mox Jasper, but in general it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:13.600
a bit reckless to alter future design space this&nbsp;
way with a type as frequently printed as dragons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:19.760
The design rule I’d set here is simple—eminence&nbsp;
abilities should not directly give cards or mana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:24.240
Indirectly is fine—all of the eminence commander&nbsp;
abilities are frequently used to generate cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.040
mana, or both in their respective decks, but there&nbsp;
needs to be an extra step involved to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.640
the specificity and interactivity of this sort of&nbsp;
ability. Accumulating these primordial resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:39.200
is a massive part of most commander decks,&nbsp;
and giving a deck these resources totally for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:43.920
free with no extra steps gets you into really&nbsp;
dangerous territory for a deck feeling healthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:49.120
and fitting in. The Ur Dragon is considered a&nbsp;
classic pub-stomper commander because it plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:54.240
durdly battlecruiser magic in principle, but&nbsp;
its design is streamlined to simply do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:59.600
better than just about any other deck can, and&nbsp;
effortlessly receiving mana is a big part of that.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:03.760
We can also see this echoed in partner&nbsp;
commanders. Of the 5 partner commanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:08.160
that see the most competitive EDH play,&nbsp;
4 of them directly give cards or mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.560
and the 5th is used indirectly with other cards&nbsp;
to grant cards and mana. This is especially the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:16.240
case with partners because the mechanic&nbsp;
inherently grants card advantage, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:21.840
is just an amplification of a general trend. Of&nbsp;
these 5, the top two are both two-color cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:26.640
because color is yet another modulating factor&nbsp;
that determines the power of a command zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:30.160
More colors means more access to powerful tools&nbsp;
and more access to powerful resources engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:35.120
and while these are most seen in cEDH, these&nbsp;
effects of course bleed into casual as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:39.360
If we look at the three most popular commanders&nbsp;
of the past year, it’s an easy to trigger card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:44.320
draw engine, another easy to trigger card draw&nbsp;
engine, and a massive mana-granter. This is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.080
to say that these designs are bad, or that it’s&nbsp;
never okay to print cards with mana symbols or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:53.520
the words draw or land on them. It’s simply a&nbsp;
reflection that WotC should be, and probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:57.200
already knows to be, a little extra careful&nbsp;
when it comes to printing things that give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.200 --> 00:16:02.000
direct resources aimed at commander players,&nbsp;
whether Eminence is involved or otherwise.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:08.000
So, Eminence. Should it really be reprinted? My&nbsp;
answer is yes, because I think the design space is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.400
interesting. The command zone is the most unique&nbsp;
part about the commander format, and its quirks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:16.640
and whose weird little nooks and crannies of&nbsp;
possibility are endlessly fascinating to me. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:20.320
for this same reason that I sometimes find myself&nbsp;
drawn to the Partner mechanic as well, despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:25.200
disliking it on a conceptual level, and Eminence&nbsp;
is a far more interesting set of nooks to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:30.160
explored further. There are some strategies that&nbsp;
just aren’t as powerful in commander, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:33.760
nice to be able to print a card that gives them a&nbsp;
boost without needing to fit the restrictions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:38.480
a conventional commander. There are also lots of&nbsp;
interesting design possibilities that don’t work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:42.960
without an element that operates with a commander&nbsp;
in the command zone, ripe design territory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:47.360
exploration. So, for the rest of this video I want&nbsp;
to think about some eminence designs that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:52.640
cool usage of the mechanic while avoiding most of&nbsp;
the elements that other people find problematic.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:56.480
Earlier in this video I’ve gone through three&nbsp;
rules I’d follow when designing Eminence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:00.960
cards—stick to two colors or less if there’s&nbsp;
any risk of competing with existing commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:06.080
aim for higher specificity if there’s any power&nbsp;
concern, and avoid any eminence effect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:10.720
directly creates card or mana advantage. I’ll&nbsp;
also add a fourth one here that I didn’t cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:17.840
earlier—Eminence commanders should be desirable&nbsp;
to actually cast. The two biggest offenders here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:23.280
are the aforementioned Edgar Markov, as well as&nbsp;
one I haven’t talked about as much—Inalla. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:27.520
issue with both of these is that the text on the&nbsp;
creature just isn’t that great of an amplification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:31.840
of the eminence effect. There are an endless list&nbsp;
of better things you could do with 1/1 vampires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:37.280
for 6 mana than putting a +1/+1 counter on them,&nbsp;
and tapping Wizards to drain opponents is an even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:43.120
worse amplification on Inalla’s value-crunching,&nbsp;
ETB-focused eminence ability. So that’ll be our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:48.080
final rule—it needs to be desirable to cast&nbsp;
the creature. This is the commander format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:52.320
so you should be casting your commander! Not&nbsp;
casting an eminence commander also betrays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:56.000
the purpose of the mechanic a bit to me as&nbsp;
well—it gives an opportunity for the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:00.880
zone to offer multiple tiers of benefit, one&nbsp;
before your commander is in play and one after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:05.600
but this can’t happen if you never have a&nbsp;
reason to ascend past the first tier of power.

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:10.240
So we have our rules, but what would I do with&nbsp;
the Eminence mechanic? I mentioned Aggro a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:15.360
earlier, and that’s actually the biggest area of&nbsp;
opportunity I personally see for eminence. See,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:20.160
aggro is actually challenging on a structural&nbsp;
level with regard to commanders themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:24.240
There are a bunch of fast deck styles that are&nbsp;
only propped up by the presence of a somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.280
fragile commander, leading to glass cannon decks&nbsp;
that are capable of crushing players but fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.120
apart to two removal spells. The commander&nbsp;
allows the deck to attain a high ceiling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:37.120
but the floor of the deck typically looks&nbsp;
pathetic in the landscape of a 3 opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:42.960
40 life format, leading to a somewhat jarring play&nbsp;
pattern. What eminence does, then, is allow you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:47.040
split the difference, with the eminence effect&nbsp;
boosting a deck’s early game and overall floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:50.800
and the commander creature offering a way to&nbsp;
compound that advantage in order to keep up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:56.160
bit later in the game. Arahbo is one example, but&nbsp;
here’s another that I came up with with a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.160 --> 00:19:01.040
ability format—instead of a conventional voltron&nbsp;
commander you dump all your resources onto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:04.800
how about an Eminence ability that reads&nbsp;
“At the beginning of combat on your turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:09.440
you may choose a creature you control. If you&nbsp;
do, put a +1/+1 counter on it for each equipment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:14.880
attached to it.” Then the creature itself could&nbsp;
be Storg, a 5 mana red black human soldier with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:18.400
the text “Whenever you attack, you may have&nbsp;
target attacking creature you control deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.360
damage equal to its power to any target. If seven&nbsp;
or more damage is dealt this way, draw a card.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:28.480
The eminence effect here can kick in early with&nbsp;
keywords like Living Weapon and For Mirrodin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:33.280
and it also works well alongside especially&nbsp;
low-slung equipment bearers like Goblin Gaveleer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.160
or Moria Marauder. This allows the deck to start&nbsp;
putting on pressure pretty early into the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.600
and encourages devoting more deck space to&nbsp;
low-cost stuff that might traditionally scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:46.720
poorly in the landscape of EDH. The creature&nbsp;
itself serves as a followup piece that amplifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:51.920
this once the deck reaches 5 mana, effectively&nbsp;
amplifying the damage of the presumably equipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:57.600
and buffed largest attacker and gaining card draw&nbsp;
if that source of damage was especially large.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:02.480
You could also construct something spicier more&nbsp;
along the lines of a Torbran or a Solphim. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:06.720
about an eminence effect that reads “the first&nbsp;
time a source you control deals noncombat damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:12.400
to a single permanent or player each turn, it&nbsp;
deals that much damage plus one instead.” Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:16.240
power it up and give it a condition: “as long as&nbsp;
you have permanents of at least four different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:20.240
types, whenever a source you control would deal&nbsp;
noncombat damage, it deals that much damage plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:24.800
one instead.” Ends up a little wordy once you&nbsp;
add the eminence clause, but you get the idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:28.960
For both this last example and the example&nbsp;
of Storg’s equipment-based eminence ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:33.920
care has been put into making these abilities&nbsp;
feel interactible. Multiple permanents are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:38.240
required to be in play in order for the effect&nbsp;
to trigger, unlike 4 of the 6 original eminence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:42.960
commanders whose ability is on a cast trigger, an&nbsp;
ETB trigger, or an upkeep trigger. Sure, you still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:46.560
can’t remove the source of the effect with these&nbsp;
ideas, but there are certainly steps required to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:52.160
get the benefit from it, and steps your opponents&nbsp;
can take to deny you that value. These also both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:57.680
fall off big time if you don’t cast the commander.&nbsp;
1 extra conditional damage isn’t that great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:02.640
and a handful of +1/+1 counters each turn that&nbsp;
you have to work for, and which prevent you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:06.960
from using one of the much better equipment&nbsp;
commanders out there, also isn’t that great.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:11.840
Aggro is the biggest area that I like personally&nbsp;
as a way to utilize eminence due to the mechanic’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:17.040
early-game emphasis and stair-steppy nature, but&nbsp;
that’s far from the only cool use case for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:21.920
There’s a lot of other interesting design niches&nbsp;
to be explored, and 3/3 Elk showed me a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:26.640
of custom cards they’d made to demonstrate them.&nbsp;
You could have an eminence ability offer a less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:31.600
concrete resource such as goad, and then have&nbsp;
the commander itself give a way to more tangibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:36.000
capitalize on goaded creatures. You could have&nbsp;
an eminence ability that accumulates resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:41.360
in some way, but with those resources only being&nbsp;
accessible by keeping the actual card in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:45.680
sort of like a riff on experience counters&nbsp;
that’s more stable and less swingy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:50.640
You could also have an eminence ability that’s&nbsp;
actually a downside, with a strong commander that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:55.920
mitigates that downside, allowing for some truly&nbsp;
unique designs and play patterns. Alternatively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.920 --> 00:22:00.480
you could even have a Breena-style politics-y&nbsp;
eminence ability that can help any player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.480
with the attached commander creature allowing a&nbsp;
player to break parity and amplify the benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:08.800
they gain from it. All of these show the potential&nbsp;
of the mechanic—it doesn’t have to be just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:13.920
powerful boost to a creature type with an optional&nbsp;
attached creature. All sorts of cool and elegant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:19.760
designs are possible here, and attaining them&nbsp;
requires, above all else, mindset and restraint.

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:23.840
I believe the Ur Dragon and Edgar Markov were each&nbsp;
made with the idea for them to be a super cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:28.000
powerful perfectly-rounded commander for players&nbsp;
who like the extremely popular tribes of Dragons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:33.040
and Vampires, respectively. This is how you create&nbsp;
the two most popular commanders of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:37.760
and in the process create cards that have each&nbsp;
commanded triple digit price tags for multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:42.320
month spans since the time they were released.&nbsp;
I also believe these cards were mistakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:47.760
but that’s because crowning a powerful color-soup&nbsp;
champion for two popular and iconic creature types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:53.600
while simultaneously introducing a spicy new&nbsp;
command zone modification was an inherently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:59.840
challenging task, and one that just so happened to&nbsp;
be a poor pairing for reasons I laid out earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:04.880
A lot of people have called eminence itself&nbsp;
a mistake or a bad mechanic, and those people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:10.320
are half-correct—eminence is a mechanic that is&nbsp;
challenging to implement well, and which carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:16.720
the full possibility to easily veer off course&nbsp;
and into problems. But importantly, that’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:21.520
because the mechanic poses such a fundamental&nbsp;
shakeup to the structure of the command zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:26.960
and that feature of it also imbues it with a ton&nbsp;
of rich gameplay possibilities. One of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:32.640
restrictive things about the commander format is&nbsp;
that decks often feel “on” or “off”—either their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:38.160
commander is in play, or it isn’t. The eminence&nbsp;
mechanic offers the ability to bypass that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:43.680
to add massive layers of dimension to commanders,&nbsp;
particularly ones that are more aggressive. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:49.904
an incredible tool, and it’s one I’d love&nbsp;
to see used again, with care, in the future.

00:23:49.904 --> 00:23:53.120
With that said, thanks for watching, and thanks&nbsp;
to Archidekt for supporting the channel. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:58.240
I’m building decks with Archidekt, I can easily&nbsp;
add cards directly into any of my categories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:02.000
including one of my mainboard categories.&nbsp;
If you want to check out my decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:05.120
a link is in the description. I’d&nbsp;
also like to thank my supporters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:09.440
some of whom are onscreen now. Joining my&nbsp;
Patreon or becoming a YouTube member are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:13.440
easiest ways to support me in making high-effort&nbsp;
videos about the commander format and beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:17.680
and I greatly appreciate all the direct support&nbsp;
I get. With that said, I’ll see you next week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:22.080
where I’ll be discussing tax evasion,&nbsp;
commander style. I’m coming for you Yuriko.

